{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792316005,"nonce":"bgqLo8qdYnbFZT4ukZ9GopXkE8Y=","title":"The banner in a window animates itself and nobody can steer it: laggy in a page","message":"The user's report: the demo is very laggy in the browser, and in the browser the user should control it, not have it animate by itself.\n\n**What was measured**\n\nIn a page at the user's screen scale, a frame took 90 to 160 ms: layout, display list and the GPU wait were a few milliseconds, and the rest was the program encoding the frame, with the agent taking one or two. The window's ticker advanced the scene's clock every frame, so every canvas (the gauges, battery, tyres, climate, energy and map, nearly all of it text and paths) redrew every frame, and the ticker also set the camera's distance every frame, which would have overridden any zoom. Natively the same scene is about 26 ms of encoding and 20 ms of presenting at a 2560 by 1640 window.\n\nA canvas that draws text costs most of it: `ctx.text` tessellates a glyph's outline each time (the RayDE session measured 44 ms a frame for 473 runs).\n\n**Fix**\n\nIn a window the scene is at rest. Nothing is redrawn until something changes, an idle window draws no frames, and the user turns the camera round the car by dragging and comes nearer or goes further by scrolling, which `\u003cscene-kit\u003e` already does: the overlay canvas for the callouts' lines and the chips are transparent to the pointer now, so a drag reaches the scene. A drag redraws only the scene and the callouts: 3 to 5 ms a frame in a page.\n\n**Open**\n\n`DrawContext.text` caching a glyph's tessellation, or drawing from the glyph atlas, would make a canvas cheap to animate. The gauges would then animate in a page.","files":null}]}